      "writtenOn": "2016-08-10T16:04:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What we can do here is have three separate migration repositories. We can keep them all in lock step and write a unit test to make sure all three repositories stay in sync. For example: \n\n  keystone/common/sql/migrate_repo/expand/\n    001_expand_project_foo_column.py\n    002_expand_user_bar_column.py\n    003_expand_new_baz_table.py\n  keystone/common/sql/migrate_repo/migrate/\n    001_populate_project_foo_column.py\n    002_migrate_data_to_new_user_bar_column.py\n    003_populate_baz_table.py\n  keystone/common/sql/migrate_repo/contract/\n    001_contract_old_project_bar_column.py\n    002_contract_old_user_baz_column.py\n    003_contract_noop.py\n\nWith this each migration will consist of three different migration scripts. Even if a migration doesn\u0027t require a contact or a migration, it could consist of something as minimal as:\n\n    def upgrade(migrate_engine):\n        pass",
      "parentUuid": "9ad45d7e_63bdd0b6",
      "revId": "25dccc02505f4d828c21c54963383802f4393ef7",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
